\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Interdisciplinarity and compositionality of cybersecurity modelling}

The development of cybersecurity models are increasingly more important in today's landscape of technological interconnectivity and dependence. It's a modeller's responsibility is to construct an accurate representation of attack and defensive scenarios, from DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) attacks on smart grid networks, to intrusion detection and response systems in distributed computer networks. Developing these models often requires the unification, interoperability, and integration of different software tools and formal methodologies. For example, \cite{Phetmanee2024}, \cite{EGTAOnline} and \cite{ccyberbattle} present modelling workflows that incorporate distinct modelling software such as Gambit (\cite{gambit}) and CyberBattleSim (\cite{msft:cyberbattlesim}), analysis techniques such as rational verification or model-checking, and automatic data integration from different sources such as Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) from the National Vulnerability Database (\hyperref{https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln}). The interdisciplinarity of these methodologies ultimately leads to complex and rigid models, with a dense network of coalescing knowledge bases and expertise \cite{e.CalibratingCollaborationInterdisciplinarity2025}. 

An avenue for addressing complex modelling problems is leveraging \textit{compositionality}. As set forth by a research agenda of the Topos Institute on collaborative modelling (\cite{lynchDeclarativeModelsCollaborative2024}), is there a way to systematically determine inherent relationships between cybersecurity models, thereby preserving analysis reproducibility and enabling model reusability? Then maybe these ideas could lead to a concept of \textit{declarative models} for cybersecurity. For a recent (unrelated to the Topos Institute research agenda) example for composable security models, \cite{caulfieldMetamodellingEcosystemsSecurity2022} develops a collaborative modelling design process (as seen in Figure \ref{figure:Fig. 1}), which describes an overarching theme of the metamodelling design process. Beyond developing a system to just construct security games from smaller ones, the model formulation process should be an \textbf{iterative} and \textbf{experiential} effort. 

Perhaps if we can identify the roles of the practitioners that contribute to the development of a model, stratify the modelling process across differing analyses and tools used, and leverage inspirations from compositionality, we can explore new techniques for cybersecurity modelling. For this thesis, we explore how to improve the cybersecurity modelling process by making modelling and verification tools modular and interoperable. We investigate two distinct cybersecurity modelling techniques. First, we bridge formal verification techniques with a simulation modelling software. Second, we provide methods for using compositional game theory for creating game-theoretic cybersecurity games. We will see how both benefit from an iterative model formulation process. 


\subsection{Game-theoretic modelling as a software architecture problem}

Game-theoretic models have been widely accepted as a tool for enhancing the defensive capabilities of a system. By modelling the interaction between an attacker and defender, where the players are rational actors maximizing their utility, a modeller can derive an optimal defensive strategy or predict an attacker strategy. Using these findings, a security administrator can deploy countermeasures preemptively, or evaluate real-time adjustments to a defensive strategy. Indeed, \cite{kiennertSurveyGameTheoreticApproaches2018} details a plethora of different models that optimize IDS performance, each considering different network topologies or attacker capabilities. The main idea is to expressively derive these security games from smaller games using compositional game theory. The first step to take is towards developing an answer to an auxiliary question: how do we develop the "correct" models? Or more tractably, which models are extensions or improvements of one another? The interpretation and creation of these models lack a systematic, conceptual \textit{feedback} loop to actively re-interpret them, with fast-changing technologies and security needs.  

Tackling a complex problem by decomposing it into bite-sized pieces is a core methodological approach in software engineering. A software engineer needs to design the correct abstractions for composing, removing, and extending blocks of code that can be conceived in isolation but ultimately brought together for a complete solution to a problem. On top of that, maintaining a code-base can often be a collaborative effort, with ever-changing requirements and unforseen bugs that stretches across different modules. Some solutions to maintaining the efficiency and stability of the code-base typically involve continuous integration, design patterns (i.e. Gang of Four), or perhaps just some human-readable variable names. 

Let's try to generalize the software engineering process as follows: a software engineer observes the problem, hypothesizes a solution and lists assumptions, they prototype an implementation as some program, tests the solution, observes the output of the program to verify assumptions, and adjusts the implementation accordingly.

Similarly, the transition from real-world phenomena to a game-theoretic model requires observing the real-world, developing abstractions according to standard game-theoretic concepts, and observe the behaviour of the game under certain conditions. Evaluation metrics include observing the respective payoffs of players under certain conditions, or determining whether a strategy profile is a Nash equilibrium. The task is left to collate these metrics and derive expectations of the players' as rational and utility-maximizing. Indeed \textit{mechanism design} is specifically concerned with sculpting these games to achieve a hypothesized outcome under certain conditions. 

Drawing from \cite{Zahn2024}, compositional game theory has enabled a strong correspondence between game-theoretic models and code. With the proper abstractions for piecing models together, developing a library of economic models that can be easily loaded and run as for simulations is within grasp. Focusing in again on "models as code", we can naturally correspond software engineering principles to game-theoretic modelling principles. The practical implications of compositional game theory were first explored in \cite[section 2.3.12]{HedgesThesis}, developing the first steps to compositionally designing problems. Constructing games for these problems using string diagrams ultimately transitioned to an implementation of the category of games as a Haskell library. Hence, we have a mechanical process that can be used to assist a modeler to understand a problem in terms of compositional game theory. However, there has yet to be a more explicit methodology for effectively using the tool, specifically outside microeconomic analysis. Rather than taking a domain-constrained approach to the tool, this thesis seeks to identify techniques using this DSL as part of a larger software-development process in order to build effective models. This is not unlike the clear benefits of simulation model reuse (\cite{robinsonSimulationModelReuse2004}), such as reducing time, cost, and reimplementation overhead for "building better models"

By modularizing this game formulation process, we can create greater interoperability and rapid model adjustment. Essentially by inverting the process by starting at simple components, we allow for generalization to a security situation through parameterization to \textit{grow} the model, rather than \textit{restrict} a security scenario to fit the confines of the game. Toward this goal, compositional game theory is in need of a clear role in a wider platform serving as a sandbox to test out new ideas for developing a library of compatible cybersecurity models.


